{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"set-up-hr-function-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Set up the HR function","description":"Guides a small company setting up HR for the first time in gated steps, from a baseline audit to records and contracts, core policies, lifecycle processes and an operating rhythm.","category":"hr-operations","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["discover","plan","build","operate"],"role":["founder","operations-manager","executive"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","document"],"output":["report","plan","checklist","questions"],"risk":"read-only","advice_risk":["legal"],"invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","tags":["first-hr-hire","hr-foundations","employee-records","hr-processes","growing-company"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["write-employee-handbook","design-probation-review-process","plan-employee-offboarding","select-hr-system","plan-hr-annual-calendar"],"personas":["hr-business-partner"]},"args":[{"name":"company","description":"Headcount now and in a year, countries or states where people work, worker types (employees, contractors, interns), industry, and who handles HR today.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"current_state","description":"What exists today, such as contracts, a handbook, payroll provider, where employee files live, any open disputes or recent problems, and what triggered this work.","type":"text","required":true}],"steps":[{"id":"baseline","file":"steps/01-baseline-audit.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"hr-setup/01-baseline.md"},{"id":"records","file":"steps/02-records-and-contracts.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"hr-setup/02-records-and-contracts.md"},{"id":"policies","file":"steps/03-core-policies.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"hr-setup/03-core-policies.md"},{"id":"lifecycle","file":"steps/04-lifecycle-processes.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"hr-setup/04-lifecycle-processes.md"},{"id":"rhythm","file":"steps/05-operating-rhythm.md","stage":"operate","gate":"none","artifact":"hr-setup/05-operating-rhythm.md"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Takes a small company from \"the founder does HR in spreadsheets\" to a working HR foundation, in the order an experienced first HR hire would: find out what exists and what is exposed, fix records and contracts, put the few essential policies in place, design the processes that run from hire to exit, then set a calendar and a handful of measures. Each step writes one artifact and stops for approval, because what the user finds (a missing contract, a misclassified contractor) changes what comes next.\n\n<company>\n{{company}}\n</company>\n\n<current_state>\n{{current_state}}\n</current_state>\n\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n\nRules for every step:\n- Use only what the user tells you. Never invent laws, thresholds, deadlines or what their documents say; mark gaps [X] and ask.\n- Every point that depends on employment, tax or data protection law is marked \"confirm locally\" and carried to a running list of questions for an employment lawyer or adviser.\n- Fix exposure first: missing contracts, unpaid entitlements, misclassified workers, right-to-work gaps and unsecured personal data come before nice-to-have programmes.\n- Keep it proportionate to the headcount. A 15-person company needs a few clear basics, not an enterprise HR model.\n- If the user mentions an open dispute, claim, harassment or safety issue, pause the step, say it needs prompt specialist attention, and continue only once they confirm it is in hand.\n- End each artifact with open questions and what the user should bring to the next step.","steps":[{"id":"baseline","file":"steps/01-baseline-audit.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"hr-setup/01-baseline.md","text":"# Step 1: Baseline audit\n\nFind out what exists and where the company is exposed before building anything.\n\n1. People inventory: how many people by country, worker type and contract type, with gaps marked. Flag anyone whose status may be misclassified (for example a \"contractor\" working fixed hours under direction), as a point to confirm locally.\n2. Document check: for each area (contracts, offer letters, handbook or policies, payroll records, leave records, right-to-work or work permit checks, personnel files, data protection notices to staff), say what exists, what is missing and how urgent it is.\n3. Exposure list: the five to eight gaps that carry the most legal, financial or people risk, ranked, with why.\n4. Quick wins: two or three fixes that take under a week.\n\nSections: People inventory (table), Document check (table: Area | Exists? | Gap | Urgency), Exposure list, Quick wins, Questions for an employment lawyer, What to bring next. Stop for approval."},{"id":"records","file":"steps/02-records-and-contracts.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"hr-setup/02-records-and-contracts.md","text":"# Step 2: Records and contracts\n\nPut the foundations in order from the approved baseline.\n\n1. Employee record: the fields to hold for each person (identity and contact, contract terms, pay, leave, emergency contact, right-to-work evidence where required), where they live, who can access them, and what not to collect. Mark retention periods \"confirm locally\".\n2. Contracts: which people need a written contract or statement of terms now, the clauses to check exist (role, pay, hours, place of work, leave, notice, probation, confidentiality, IP), and a plan to issue or update them. This is a checklist for a lawyer or template provider, not a drafted contract.\n3. Worker status: for any flagged misclassification, the facts to gather and the question to put to an adviser.\n4. Data protection: a staff privacy notice, access control on HR files and a secure place for sensitive data, all to confirm against local rules.\n\nSections: Employee record (table: Field | Why | Access | Retention to confirm), Contracts plan, Worker status questions, Data protection actions, Questions for an employment lawyer, What to bring next. Stop for approval."},{"id":"policies","file":"steps/03-core-policies.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"hr-setup/03-core-policies.md","text":"# Step 3: Core policies\n\nChoose the few policies the company needs now, and in what order.\n\n1. Must-haves: list the policies commonly expected or required for a company of this size and location (for example leave and absence, conduct and discipline, grievance, anti-harassment, health and safety, data protection for staff, whistleblowing where required), each marked \"required or expected, confirm locally\".\n2. For each, the decisions the company must make before drafting (for example sick pay above the legal minimum, remote work rules) and who decides.\n3. Order of work and owner, with the handbook as the container for most of them.\n4. How policies will be communicated, acknowledged and reviewed.\n\nDo not draft full policies here; point to the handbook and policy prompts for drafting. Sections: Policy list (table: Policy | Why now | Decisions needed | Owner | Due), Communication and review, Questions for an employment lawyer, What to bring next. Stop for approval."},{"id":"lifecycle","file":"steps/04-lifecycle-processes.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"hr-setup/04-lifecycle-processes.md","text":"# Step 4: Lifecycle processes\n\nDesign the processes that run from hire to exit, lightly enough for the company's size.\n\n1. Hire to start: offer, contract issue, right-to-work check where required, payroll and system set-up, first-day plan.\n2. Probation and early reviews: checkpoints and who owns them.\n3. Everyday: leave requests and approval, absence reporting, changes to terms, expenses.\n4. Performance and pay: when reviews happen and how pay decisions are made and recorded.\n5. Concerns: how an employee raises a concern and who handles it independently.\n6. Exit: resignation handling, final pay items to confirm, access removal and records.\n\nFor each, a short flow with owner, the record kept and the tool used (spreadsheet or system). Sections: one subsection per process, then Tools needed, Questions for an employment lawyer, What to bring next. Stop for approval."},{"id":"rhythm","file":"steps/05-operating-rhythm.md","stage":"operate","gate":"none","artifact":"hr-setup/05-operating-rhythm.md","text":"# Step 5: Operating rhythm\n\nMake HR run on a schedule rather than from memory.\n\n1. Twelve-month calendar: reviews, pay review, benefits renewals, policy reviews, statutory filings to confirm, training.\n2. Four to six measures leadership will see (for example headcount, voluntary turnover, first-year leavers, absence rate, time to hire), each with a one-line definition.\n3. When to buy an HR system and when to hire or outsource HR help, with the signals that trigger each.\n4. The consolidated list of every \"confirm locally\" point from steps 1 to 4, grouped for one meeting with an employment lawyer or adviser.\n\nSections: Calendar (table), Measures, Tools and help, Consolidated questions for an employment lawyer, Next 90 days. This is the final artifact."}]}
